<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Possession with <em>have got</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Recognize and use the conversational possession pattern in <span class="ex">I’ve got a car</span>, <span class="ex">She’s got two sisters</span>, and <span class="ex">Have you got your phone?</span> Then connect it to the ordinary U.S. forms <span class="ex">I have</span> and <span class="ex">Do you have...?</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">have / has got</span>
    <span class="tag">present possession and connections</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Meet two people and their things.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Choose have or has.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Make questions, negatives, and short answers.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Complete a fictional resource check.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Have got</span> is one conversational way to present what someone owns, what relationships or features they have, and what condition is true now. Ordinary <span class="ex">have</span> gives the same possession meaning and is the neutral default in U.S. English.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved third-person present-simple works and matched there is or there are to one sofa and two windows.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Answer from memory, then check. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="works">works</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="work">work</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There is">There is</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There are">There are</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Maria’s regular work profile says she <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="works" data-hint="A regular action with the third-person subject Maria takes works." data-fix="Maria works in a bank." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> in a bank. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(present simple)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The room inventory introduces one sofa: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="There is" data-hint="A sofa is one countable thing, so use There is." data-fix="There is a sofa in the living room." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a sofa in the living room. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(there is / there are)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The office inventory introduces two windows: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="There are" data-hint="Two windows is plural, so use There are." data-fix="There are two windows in the office." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> two windows in the office. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(there is / there are)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Two profiles</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"I’ve got a small apartment and a bicycle. My friend Rosa has got a house. She’s got two children, but she hasn’t got a car."</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which subject uses <span class="ex">has got</span>?</li>
      <li>Find the negative form.</li>
      <li>What does <span class="ex">’ve</span> mean?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Have matches I, you, and we; has matches he and it; and each negative contracts not with the subject-matched form.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the pattern</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the subject to choose <b>have</b> or <b>has</b>.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has">has</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="haven’t">haven’t</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hasn’t">hasn’t</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Our confirmed calendar shows a meeting at 10. We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="The subject we takes have before got." data-fix="We have got a meeting at 10." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got a meeting at 10.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional equipment profile says he owns a laptop. He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="The subject he takes has before got." data-fix="He has got a laptop." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got a laptop.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The cash check shows my wallet is empty. I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="haven’t" data-hint="The subject I takes have; contract have not to haven't." data-fix="I haven't got any cash." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got any cash.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Maya’s schedule is full, so she cannot add another call. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hasn’t" data-hint="The subject she takes has; contract has not to hasn't." data-fix="She hasn't got time for another call." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got time for another call.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>I sent the contact card yesterday. You <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="The subject you takes have before got." data-fix="You have got my number already." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got my number already.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The pronoun <span class="ex">it</span> refers to one fictional apartment. It <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="The singular subject it takes has before got." data-fix="It has got a small garden." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got a small garden.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: have got and has got</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">have got</span> with <strong>I / you / we / they</strong>. Use <span class="ex">has got</span> with <strong>he / she / it</strong>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>I / you / we / they</th><th>he / she / it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>positive</b></td><td>We have got a car.</td><td>She has got a car.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>negative</b></td><td>We haven’t got one.</td><td>She hasn’t got one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>question</b></td><td>Have you got one?</td><td>Has she got one?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Short answers: <span class="ex">Yes, I have. / No, she hasn’t.</span> Do not repeat <span class="ex">got</span> in a short answer.</p>

  <h2>Get to the root: a present connection</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Have got</span> describes a connection that is true now: something belongs to you, is part of your life, or is one of your characteristics. In <span class="ex">I’ve got a bicycle</span>, <span class="ex">got</span> does not mean “received” and does not make the sentence past. The whole chunk means “I have a bicycle now.”</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>kind of connection</th><th>example</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>possession</b></td><td>I’ve got a laptop.</td><td>The laptop belongs to me.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>relationship</b></td><td>She’s got two brothers.</td><td>They are part of her family.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>feature</b></td><td>The apartment has got a balcony.</td><td>The balcony is part of it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>condition / need</b></td><td>I’ve got a headache. We’ve got a problem.</td><td>This is our present situation.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Why have and has do the grammar work</h3>
  <p>In this structure, <span class="ex">have / has</span> behaves like an auxiliary. It agrees with the subject, moves to the front for questions, and combines directly with <span class="ex">not</span>. <span class="ex">Got</span> stays unchanged.</p>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Follow the moving part</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">She has got a car. → Has she got a car? → She hasn’t got a car.</span></p>
    <p>Only <span class="ex">has</span> moves or becomes negative. This is why <span class="ex">Does she has got…?</span> mixes two different systems and is not the target form.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each contracted chunk matches its subject and the stated positive or negative present connection.">
    <h3>Drill 1: choose the natural chunk</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the subject first. Then choose the contracted chunk people really say.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="’ve got">’ve got</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="’s got">’s got</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="haven’t got">haven’t got</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="hasn’t got">hasn’t got</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional profile says, “I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’ve got" data-hint="The subject I takes have; contract I have to I've before got." data-fix="I've got two sisters." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> two sisters.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The calendar shows three meetings for one manager. My boss <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’s got" data-hint="The singular subject my boss takes has; contract has to 's before got." data-fix="My boss has got three meetings today." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> three meetings today.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The fictional home-office inventory lists no printer. We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="haven’t got" data-hint="The subject we takes have; the inventory gives a negative result." data-fix="We haven't got a printer in the home office." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a printer in the home office.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The service list says Wi-Fi is unavailable. The café <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hasn’t got" data-hint="The singular subject the café takes has, and unavailable requires the negative." data-fix="The café hasn't got Wi-Fi, so I work at the library." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Wi-Fi, so I work at the library.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The apartment profile gives a couple one large kitchen. They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’ve got" data-hint="The subject they takes have; contract they have to they've." data-fix="They've got a big kitchen." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a big kitchen.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The device specification lists one high-quality camera. The phone <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’s got" data-hint="The singular subject the phone takes has; contract has to 's." data-fix="The phone has got a good camera." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a good camera.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>I already sent the contact card. You <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="’ve got" data-hint="The subject you takes have; contract you have to you've." data-fix="You've got my email address already." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> my email address already.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The fictional schedule has no open time this week. He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="hasn’t got" data-hint="The subject he takes has; no open time requires the negative hasn't got." data-fix="He hasn't got time for another meeting this week." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> time for another meeting this week.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2><em>Have got</em> and <em>have</em></h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>For a present possession or connection, both systems below are correct. <span class="ex">Have got</span> is especially common in British English, but learners also hear <span class="ex">I've got</span> and similar forms in American conversation. In neutral U.S. English, ordinary <span class="ex">have</span> is usually the clearest default, especially in questions and negatives.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>have got system</th><th>ordinary have system</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>I’ve got a car.</td><td>I have a car.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Have you got a car?</td><td>Do you have a car?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>I haven’t got a car.</td><td>I don’t have a car.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Choose one system inside a clause. Use <span class="ex">Have you got a car?</span> or <span class="ex">Do you have a car?</span>, never <span class="ex">Do you have got a car?</span> Both correct systems communicate the same present possession meaning.</p>

  <h3>Sound natural: contractions, linking, and focus</h3>
  <p>In speech, positive <span class="ex">have got</span> forms are usually contracted: <span class="ex">I’ve got, you’ve got, he’s got, we’ve got</span>. Here <span class="ex">he’s</span> means <span class="ex">he has</span>, not <span class="ex">he is</span>, because it is followed by <span class="ex">got</span>. Negative forms commonly contract too: <span class="ex">haven’t got, hasn’t got</span>.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural in U.S. English</h3>
    <p>Link the contracted subject to <span class="ex">got</span>: <span class="ex">I've-got a charger</span> and <span class="ex">she's-got two sisters</span>. In a neutral statement, the new or contrasting noun often carries the strongest beat: <span class="ex">I've got a <strong>CHAR</strong>ger</span>. In the question <span class="ex">Have-you-got a charger?</span>, the small words link while <span class="ex">charger</span> stays clear.</p>
    <p>In ordinary U.S. <span class="ex">Do you have...?</span>, speakers often reduce and link the first words: <span class="ex">D'you-have a charger?</span> Keep the full words in writing, and use the careful form whenever the listener needs more clarity.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Possession is not an activity</h3>
    <p>Use <span class="ex">have got</span> for possession and present connections, not for the action meaning of <span class="ex">have</span>. Say <span class="ex">I have lunch at one every day</span> when <span class="ex">have</span> means “eat.” <span class="ex">I've got lunch at one</span> can instead suggest that a lunch appointment is on your schedule, so the context changes the meaning.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>present connection</th><th>activity</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="ex">I've got a car.</span><br />The car belongs to me now.</td><td><span class="ex">I have lunch at one.</span><br /><span class="ex">Have</span> means “eat.”</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="ex">She's got a meeting at one.</span><br />The meeting is on her schedule.</td><td><span class="ex">She's having lunch now.</span><br />Lunch is the open activity.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Present possession, past possession, and U.S. <em>gotten</em></h3>
    <p><span class="ex">I've got a car</span> describes possession now. For an ordinary past possession, use <span class="ex">I had a car last year</span>. Do not use present <span class="ex">have got</span> with a finished past time such as <span class="ex">last year</span>.</p>
    <p>In U.S. English, <span class="ex">have gotten</span> is a different, later pattern. It can describe receiving or becoming: <span class="ex">I've gotten three messages today</span> and <span class="ex">It's gotten colder</span>. Do not add <span class="ex">-en</span> to the possession pattern in this lesson.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read have got and ordinary have listening check">
    <h2>Listen: possession, U.S. alternative, or activity?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each complete fictional context once at a natural speed and once more slowly. Listen for the subject, the chosen possession system, and whether <span class="ex">have</span> names an activity. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You heard subject agreement, a negative and question, the ordinary U.S. possession system, and the activity meaning of have.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Listening form choices">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="I’ve got">I’ve got</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="She’s got">She’s got</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="We haven’t got">We haven’t got</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Have you got">Have you got</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Do you have">Do you have</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The speaker reports a charger in their fictional kit: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="I’ve got" data-hint="Listen for the subject I and the contracted have-got possession form." data-fix="I've got a charger in my kit." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> a charger in my kit.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The profile gives Maya a current family relationship: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="She’s got" data-hint="Listen for the subject she and the contracted has-got form." data-fix="She's got two sisters." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> two sisters.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The group reports that its resource kit contains no cash: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="We haven’t got" data-hint="Listen for the plural subject we and the negative have-got form." data-fix="We haven't got any cash." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> any cash.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The host uses the have-got question system to check one reservation: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Have you got" data-hint="The requested have-got question moves Have before you." data-fix="Have you got a reservation?" data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> a reservation?</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The U.S. service worker uses the ordinary-have system to ask about identification: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Do you have" data-hint="The ordinary-have question begins Do you have and contains no got." data-fix="Do you have an ID?" data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> an ID?</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The routine describes eating at noon, not possessing a meal: I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="Here have means eat, so use ordinary present-simple have without got." data-fix="I have lunch at noon every weekday." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button> lunch at noon every weekday.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details>
      <summary>Teacher: open the script after the learner checks</summary>
      <ol>
        <li>I've got a charger in my kit.</li>
        <li>She's got two sisters.</li>
        <li>We haven't got any cash.</li>
        <li>Have you got a reservation?</li>
        <li>Do you have an ID?</li>
        <li>I have lunch at noon every weekday.</li>
      </ol>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each question begins with subject-matched Have or Has, and each short answer keeps that auxiliary without repeating got.">
    <h3>Drill 2: type the question word or short answer</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use <b>have</b> with I, you, we, they. Use <b>has</b> with he, she, it. Short answers never repeat <b>got</b>.</p>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A travel checklist asks about one passport: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Have" data-hint="The subject you takes have; move Have to the front and capitalize it." data-fix="Have you got a passport?" aria-label="Have-got question starter before you got a passport" /> you got a passport?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional transport profile uses <span class="ex">he</span> for Leo: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Has" data-hint="The subject Leo takes has; move Has to the front and capitalize it." data-fix="Has Leo got a car?" aria-label="Have-got question starter before Leo got a car" /> Leo got a car?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The same profile says Leo has no car. “Has Leo got a car?” “No, he <input class="answer-input" data-answer="hasn’t" data-hint="The negative short answer to Has he...? keeps has and contracts not." data-fix="No, he hasn't." aria-label="Negative short answer after No he for Has Leo got a car" />.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>A receptionist asks your fictional group, “Have you got the keys?” The group answers, “Yes, we <input class="answer-input" data-answer="have" data-hint="The positive short answer to Have you...? uses have after we and omits got." data-fix="Yes, we have." aria-label="Positive short answer after Yes we for Have you got the keys" />.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A fictional family profile uses the plural subject <span class="ex">the parents</span>: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Have" data-hint="The plural subject the parents takes have; move Have to the front." data-fix="Have the parents got a house?" aria-label="Have-got question starter before the parents got a house" /> the parents got a house?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The profile says the parents have no house. “Have they got a house?” “No, they <input class="answer-input" data-answer="haven’t" data-hint="The negative short answer to Have they...? keeps have and contracts not." data-fix="No, they haven't." aria-label="Negative short answer after No they for Have they got a house" />.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-answers>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-answers>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read and choose</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A message from a friend</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"Hi! We’ve got the keys to the new apartment. It’s got two bedrooms and a small balcony. We haven’t got a table yet, so we eat in the kitchen. Marco’s got a car, so moving day is easy. Have you got time on Saturday to help us?"</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You located the apartment's two bedrooms, missing table, and Marco's car from explicit details in the message.">
    <h3>Drill 3: read, then choose</h3>
