  <h2>Notice and repair errors</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="have" data-hint="The fictional profile names one person, my brother, so choose the he form." data-fix="My brother has got a new job." data-why="Use has got with he, she, or a singular person.">
    <h3>Drill 6: one person, one form</h3>
    <p>A fictional profile says “My brother have got a new job.” Tap the word that is wrong.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="brother">My brother</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="have">have</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="got">got</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="job">a new job.</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="got" data-hint="The instruction requires the ordinary U.S. have system, so Do you have is already complete." data-fix="Do you have a reservation?" data-why="The ordinary-have question uses do plus the base form have and contains no got.">
    <h3>Drill 7: one system at a time</h3>
    <p>The host must use the ordinary U.S. have system: “Do you have got a reservation?” Tap the extra word.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="do">Do</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="you">you</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="have">have</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="got">got</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="reservation">a reservation?</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-transform>
    <h3>Drill 8: say it, then reveal</h3>
    <p>Read the cue, say the full sentence aloud, then reveal only after you answer.</p>
    <div class="transform-list">
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> I’ve got a ticket. Change to she.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>She’s got a ticket.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> They’ve got time. Make it negative.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>They haven’t got time.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> She’s got a car. Ask the question.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Has she got a car?</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> Has he got a pen? Answer yes.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Yes, he has.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> We’ve got a meeting. Change to he.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>He’s got a meeting.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>6</b> I’ve got my keys. Make it negative.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>I haven’t got my keys.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it in real life</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your possessions, money, health, family, home, workplace, schedule, documents, or travel plans. Use a fictional resource kit, profile, apartment listing, or service-desk card. If a visual is not accessible, the teacher reads every relevant detail aloud.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Resource-kit check:</b> Use an invented kit list. Report three included items and two missing items: <span class="ex">We've got two chargers. We haven't got any cash.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Service desk:</b> Ask five questions from a fictional checklist. Use both <span class="ex">Have you got...?</span> and ordinary U.S. <span class="ex">Do you have...?</span>, then answer with the matching system.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Fictional profiles:</b> Describe one person's family relationship, apartment feature, useful possession, and present condition with <span class="ex">she's got</span> or <span class="ex">he's got</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Possession or activity:</b> Explain the difference between <span class="ex">She's got a lunch meeting at one</span>, <span class="ex">She has lunch at one every day</span>, and <span class="ex">She's having lunch now</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Same meaning, two systems:</b> Restate <span class="ex">They've got a printer</span> as <span class="ex">They have a printer</span>, then change both systems into a question and a negative.</div>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: prepare a first-day resource kit</h3>
    <p>Use a fictional new employee card and two possible resource kits. Give a 45- to 60-second recommendation, then complete a short service-desk exchange.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Use one accurate <span class="ex">have got</span> statement with <span class="ex">I, you, we</span>, or <span class="ex">they</span>.</li>
      <li>Use one accurate <span class="ex">has got</span> statement with a singular person or thing.</li>
      <li>Include one negative and one correctly ordered have-got question with a matching short answer.</li>
      <li>Restate one possession with the ordinary U.S. <span class="ex">have</span> system.</li>
      <li>Use ordinary <span class="ex">have</span>, not <span class="ex">have got</span>, for one routine activity.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The teacher asks one follow-up question. Repair one sentence if the subject agreement, possession system, or activity meaning is unclear.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Say the system back</h3>
    <p>Complete this aloud: “With I, you, we, and they, I use ______ got. With he, she, and it, I use ______ got. For a question, ______ moves in front of the subject. In a short answer, I never repeat ______.”</p>
    <p>Then use a fictional profile to give one positive, one negative, and one question. Restate one possession with ordinary U.S. <span class="ex">have</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You matched have or has to the subject, built questions and short answers, distinguished two possession systems, and kept activity have separate.">
    <h3>Final quiz: have got forms and meaning</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="has got" data-hint="The fictional ownership record uses the singular subject Nina, so choose has got." data-fix="Nina has got a new phone.">
